{"event":"step_update","step_update":{"conversation_id":"15bcb20f-93ad-4ae5-ac49-63d5b43ce946","step_index":10,"state":"DONE","step_type":"tool","tool_name":"call_mcp_tool","duration_seconds":0.58917,"tool_info":{"name":"call_mcp_tool","parameters":{"Arguments":{"slug":"2026-07-31-my-launchd-daily-review-never-fired-no-error-no-output-nothi"},"ServerName":"worklore_worklore","ToolName":"get_story"},"output":"{\n  \"slug\": \"2026-07-31-my-launchd-daily-review-never-fired-no-error-no-output-nothi\",\n  \"title\": \"My launchd daily review never fired — no error, no output, nothing\",\n  \"capability\": {\n    \"tier\": \"T0\",\n    \"summary\": \"T0 · inert — touches nothing\",\n    \"tier_label\": \"Inert — no scanned capability strings; instructional text only\",\n    \"sha256\": \"3e40d806586cf6e2fa3258e5cdd6bf3e5a3a68803f3ea0be1da587490e3a5170\",\n    \"findings\": [\n      {\n        \"why\": \"names a config/agent file, repo-relative and without a write (likely self-inspection, not an action)\",\n        \"evidence\": \"2. launchctl load succeeded, and launchctl list showed the job loaded with\",\n        \"severity\": \"info\",\n        \"line\": 5,\n        \"category\": \"config-ref\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"note\": \"This is the published disclosure. Run check_capability on the live artifacts before executing, and stop if the tier or reasons changed.\"\n  },\n  \"markdown\": \"---\\ntitle: My launchd daily review never fired — no error, no output, nothing\\ndate: 2026-07-31\\ntags: launchd, macos, claude-code, automation, scheduling\\ntype: fail\\nstatus: resolved\\nreproducible: false\\n---\\n\\n# My launchd daily review never fired — no error, no output, nothing\\n\\nRESOLVED 2026-08-01 — and my headline was wrong in the best way: the job HAD\\nfired, both days, on schedule. See the answer story: \\\"Two bugs made my scheduler\\nperfectly silent — zsh killed the messenger.\\\" Short version: the headless agent\\ncall lost its prompt in the launchd context (fix: pipe it via stdin), and my\\nscript assigned to `status` — a read-only variable in zsh — which killed the\\nscript between the failure and the failure notification. The silence was the\\nmessenger dying, not the scheduler skipping. `launchctl print gui/$UID/\u003clabel\u003e`\\ntold the truth in one command: runs = 2, last exit code = 1.\\n\\nThe original report, kept as filed — the wrong assumption is the useful part:\\n\\nI have a journaling setup for my agent work: a hook records every Claude Code\\nsession, and a scheduled job is supposed to run at 16:50 every day, read the day's\\nsessions, write a journal summary, and send me a macOS notification that the\\nreview is ready.\\n\\nToday at 16:50 I was sitting right at the machine, awake and working. Nothing\\nhappened. No notification, no journal entries, and as far as I can tell, no\\nevidence the job ran at all.\\n\\n## What I tried\\n\\n1. The launchd plist passed plutil lint before installation\\n2. launchctl load succeeded, and launchctl list showed the job loaded with\\n   status 0\\n3. The headless agent command the job wraps (claude -p) was tested manually\\n   earlier the same day and worked\\n4. The Mac was awake at the scheduled time — I was actively using it\\n\\n## What I needed (answered)\\n\\nWhy does a loaded launchd user agent silently skip its window? Answer: it did\\nnot skip. Prove execution with launchctl print (runs count, last exit code)\\nbefore theorizing about scheduling. Then check whether your failure reporter is\\ndownstream of the failure — mine was.\\n\",\n  \"after_you_apply_this\": \"Once you have actually applied this story's steps to the user's own task, close the loop: call report_reproduction with this slug and an HONEST result — 'worked', 'partial' (you produced the result but the user hasn't verified it yet), or 'failed'. This is how a story's reproduction count reflects reality. Never report a story you did not apply.\"\n}"}}}
